  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Given $P(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2},$ solve $P(x)>0.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $P(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & ={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}$ are $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2,$ where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=\\pm 2$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/850b1abb4ff6d9317dc8c0798f47562185cdf3b2ad42907baefda608b0667597.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).\n$$",
